{"name":"Escargot","hex":"#fff1d8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"escargot","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dish, commonly associated with French cuisine, consisting of edible snails.","A snail (often Helix pomatia) used in preparation of that dish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/escargot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Escargot (transcription factor)","blocks":[{"text":"Escargot (esg) is a transcription factor expressed in Drosophila melanogaster. It is responsible for the maintenance of intestinal stem cells and is used as a marker for those types of cells in Drosophila. Apart from its expression in the gut, esg is also expressed in germline stem cells and cyst stem cells of the testis and, during development, in neural stem cells and imaginal disks."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escargot_(transcription_factor)"},{"title":"Snails as food","blocks":[{"text":"Snails are eaten by humans in many areas such as Africa, Southeast Asia and Mediterranean Europe, while in other cultures, snails are seen as a taboo food. Borrowed from the French word for 'snail', the term escargot is what English speakers use to specifically denote edible land snails. Snails as a food date back to ancient times, with numerous cultures worldwide having traditions and practices that attest to their consumption. In the modern era snails are farmed, an industry known as heliciculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Caracoles-del-restaurante-granero.jpg/330px-Caracoles-del-restaurante-granero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snails_as_food"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Esg_phylogenic_tree.png/500px-Esg_phylogenic_tree.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Esg_phylogenic_tree.png/500px-Esg_phylogenic_tree.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Phylogenetic tree of D. melanogaster esg sequence","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEsg_phylogenic_tree.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Esg_stemness.png/500px-Esg_stemness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Esg_stemness.png/1280px-Esg_stemness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Esg_stemness.png/500px-Esg_stemness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hypothesized function of escargot in insect gut ISC - Instestinal stem cell; EE - Enteroendocrine cell","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEsg_stemness.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg/500px-Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg/1280px-Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg/500px-Canned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the 21st century, snails can be found in canned form.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanned_Snails_from_Dollarama.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Eating_Snails_in_France.jpg/500px-Eating_Snails_in_France.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Eating_Snails_in_France.